   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Progress Toward Outcome: Contracts for shipbuilding (Exeno Yamamizu Corporation), shipbuilding supervision (Braeamar), and rehabilitation of port auxiliary infrastructure (fenders) (Hall Contracting Pty Ltd) have been awarded.Fender installation is ongoing and close to completion.The progress of shipbuilding is on track and will be completed in December 2024 as scheduled. The ship will be handed over to the government in March 2025.</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Some results are in the Environmental and Social Monitoring Reports | Aug 2024: https://www.adb.org/sites/default/files/project-documents/54463/54463-001-esmr-en_1.pdf</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Status of Implementation Progress: The shipbuilding is advancing. The ship was launched in Saiki, Japan on 9 July 2024. After the launching, outfitting of the ship, testing and obtaining of certificates will be required prior to the ship delivery to the government in Saiki, Japan in Q1 2025.

Two priority areas for capacity building have been identified: (i) to develop a culture of ownership by crew and operators, extending to more regular attention to maintenance; and (ii) to increase awareness of the need for prioritized resourcing to the maritime transport sector, both in human resources and budget allocation. One of individual consultants completed on-the-job training to the Tuvaluan officers and crews who attended the major maintenance works of existing government ship. Lesson learnt will be reflected to maintenance work of the new ship.

Contract for the rehabilitation of the fenders was signed on 6 March 2023. Implementation is on track and close to completion.</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
